{"_id":"wmGMGuide0000001","name":"GM Companion","ownership":{"default":0},"pages":[{"_id":"wmGuidePage00001","name":"GM — Settings & campaign overrides","type":"text","sort":100000,"ownership":{"default":0},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>GM — Settings &amp; campaign overrides</h1><p>GM reference</p></header><p>This companion is not player-facing. Keep its compendium and imported journal restricted. The Player Guide is safe to share at Observer permission; do not grant players Owner permission to GM notes.</p><p>Open Wrathmaker's control panel in Module Settings. The experimental Workbench is default-off; enable it when ready. Material rules, gathering and Hexploration have separate controls. Disabling a feature preserves saved selections. Enhanced flanking is permanent.</p><p>Edit material names, six-tier values, prices, rarities and bonus types; edit dragon colors and resistance values separately. The standard profession rules use a single retrainable profession, Additional Lore and profession-linked Specialty Crafting. Keep the optional profession setting disabled for these rules. Refresh All in Module Settings updates module-managed content and removes retired module effects. It first saves a GM-only journal in Wrathmaker Backups, then opens a completion report listing additions, removals, changes, skipped entries and failures. Custom GM settings are retained. Complete or cancel reserved upgrades before refreshing those items. The Player Guide is a shipped default reference, not a live mirror of custom settings: annotate an imported copy when changing campaign rules.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00002","name":"GM — Resources, regions & awards","type":"text","sort":200000,"ownership":{"default":0},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>GM — Resources, regions &amp; awards</h1><p>GM reference</p></header><p>Set the gathering reward destination and the fallback environment and maximum Tier. Region-aware gathering can use supported scene/region terrain and level data; verify availability before play. Put mount and vehicle Actors in Wrathmaker Travel's Animals & Mounts and Vehicles & Transport folders.</p><p>Award Resource Units using the resource compendium. Do not halve their base prices merely to perform a half-price sale. Older Treasure documents are preserved; take care with existing project reservations before replacing stock. Apex items are campaign rewards whose prices are intentionally unspecified. Omnipotentium ingots are GM awards only, cannot be gathered, and have no standard market value. Apply the Voidborn creature trait to enable its protection. Omni weapons and active Omni-framed focuses bypass that half-damage protection; worn Omni armour halves damage from Voidborn attackers. Other resistances and immunities still apply.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00004","name":"GM — Enchanting and teamwork","type":"text","sort":400000,"ownership":{"default":0},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>GM — Enchanting and teamwork</h1><p>GM reference</p></header><h2>Enchant while crafting</h2>\n<p>Assign an Enchanting artisan to an Assistant Artisan slot, then click Enchant Item on their card. Choose a Mana Gem tier and an eligible property rune. The rune list shows descriptions; bookmarks open the full item reference. Planned runes appear in the yellow panel beneath the crafting controls. Remove a rune there to change the plan.</p>\n<p>Each rune reserves one Mana Gem of the selected tier with the project. Gems are consumed and native rune effects applied when the item is completed. The enchanter counts toward teamwork. Removing the enchanter or selecting incompatible equipment prevents the enchanted project from starting.</p>\n<h2>Enchant finished equipment</h2><p>Use Enchant at the top of the Workbench. Choose an Enchanting artisan you control, a single weapon or suit of armour, and an unreserved Mana Gem from the Party Stash. Select a rune and confirm to consume one unit immediately and apply the rune. An active GM handles shared-stash requests. Split equipment stacks first and finish any active upgrade before enchanting.</p>\n<h2>Rune limits and costs</h2><p>Mana Gem tiers 1–6 allow rune levels up to 1, 4, 8, 12, 16 and 20 respectively. Each property rune costs one resource unit, regardless of equipment size. Native equipment restrictions apply. Material attack or armour bonuses supply rune slots, capped at three; no potency rune is required. With the default progression, tiers 1–6 provide 0, 1, 2, 3, 3 and 3 slots. Improving an existing rune replaces its lower-grade version. Striking runes remain responsible for extra base weapon damage dice.</p><p>Each helper adds +1 to Work Block rolls, up to +5. This stacks with the profession bonus. Work rates remain ×1 for 1–2 artisans, ×1.25 for 3–5 and ×1.5 for six. Any critical success can trigger a Crafting Edge, including a result at least 10 above the DC. Masterstroke opportunities occur only on a qualifying final block. Roll the weapon or equipment Masterstroke table automatically, rerolling incompatible results. Each item keeps one Masterstroke through upgrades. Limited benefits apply to the first eligible roll or hit, and their uses follow the item when transferred. Overwhelming Impact offers a prone or push choice.</p></section>"}},{"_id":"wmGuidePage00003","name":"GM — Custom items, recovery & adjudication","type":"text","sort":300000,"ownership":{"default":0},"title":{"show":true,"level":1},"text":{"format":1,"content":"<section class=\"cmt-rules-handbook\"><header><h1>GM — Custom items, recovery &amp; adjudication</h1><p>GM reference</p></header><p>The GM Item Creator creates world items without project costs. Choose the Core material, tier, components and artisan name. New custom items go into GM crafted items with no player ownership. Folder visibility follows the items inside; grant access deliberately when awarding an item.</p><p>Recover Missing Item recreates a completed project's missing output without charging again, while checking for an existing output. Recovery is blocked after dismantling. Do not use recovery to award duplicates.</p><p>The module does not itself define Nephilim Point powers. Define awards and expenditures for your campaign. Back up the world before broad changes to inventory or campaign configuration.</p></section>"}}]}
